{"parentUuid":"7b1ae1bc-a749-4595-962c-f96058942e1b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01LSconAJNg3B3kciZ8RJd6s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have enough to produce the review. Here it is.\n\n## Code Review: `review-fix-progress-stream`\n\n### Scope clarification\n\nThe branch's commit history is essentially `main` (already-reviewed merged PRs). The unreviewed work is the **uncommitted working tree** plus untracked artifacts: a PTY-based streaming fix for `entire review` agents that were block-buffering stdout, surfacing all events at end-of-run instead of streaming live.\n\n### Overview\n\n- **Core fix** (`review/types/template.go`): adds `UsePTY` field to `ReviewerTemplate`. When set, stdout is routed through `creack/pty` master/slave so the child's `isatty(STDOUT_FILENO)` returns true and libc/Rust/Node revert to line-buffered flushing. Stderr keeps its own pipe; `pty.ErrUnsupported` (Windows) falls back to regular pipe; Linux `EIO` from ptmx on child exit is translated to `EOF` via a small `ptyReader` wrapper.\n- **Enables PTY** on claude-code, codex, gemini-cli reviewers.\n- **Adds debug logging** of per-event arrival times in `run.go` and `run_multi.go`.\n- **Tests**: end-to-end PTY pipe test, env-injection test, per-line streaming pin on the Claude parser.\n- **Smoke binary** under `e2e/streaming-smoke/` that self-spawns to demo bug vs fix.\n\n★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────\nThe \"block-buffering when not a TTY\" trap is universal across language runtimes (libc, Rust, Node) — PTY is the standard fix. The wrinkle here is Linux ptmx returning EIO instead of EOF when the slave closes, which would normally surface as a `bufio.Scanner.Err()` and falsely flip `Finished{Success:false}` — the `ptyReader.Read` shim handles that quietly.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n### Correctness concerns\n\n- **`appendNoDecorationEnv` is almost entirely a no-op for real runs** (`template.go:177-194`). All three agents build `cmd.Env` from `os.Environ()`, which always contains `TERM` and frequently contains `FORCE_COLOR`. The \"preserve if already set\" loop then `delete`s those keys from `want`, so only `NO_COLOR=1` is actually appended in practice. The doc comment (\"Values already set on the parent env are preserved\") accurately describes the code but obscures the practical effect. If you actually want to defang decoration, *override* `TERM` and `FORCE_COLOR` rather than respect parent values, or set them with explicit precedence (Env vars from later entries win in `exec`, so an append actually overrides — the deletion in `want` is what prevents the override). Either change the behavior or update the comment to say \"only `NO_COLOR` is added; `TERM`/`FORCE_COLOR` deliberately defer to user environment.\"\n- **`.entire/settings.json` adds `\"log_level\": \"DEBUG\"`** — CLAUDE.md explicitly says this belongs in `.entire/settings.local.json` (gitignored). Committing DEBUG into the tracked settings file will spam logs for every user of this repo. Move it before merging.\n- **Untracked 13 MB Mach-O `streaming-smoke` binary** at repo root is not in `.gitignore`. One `git add .` away from a giant accidental commit. Either delete it, add `streaming-smoke` (or `e2e/streaming-smoke/streaming-smoke`) to `.gitignore`, or have the smoke script use `go run` exclusively and clean up.\n- **Per-event `logging.Debug` in hot path** (`run.go:67-72`, `run_multi.go:156-162`): `fmt.Sprintf(\"%T\", ev)` and `slog.String/Duration` attrs are evaluated unconditionally — `slog`'s level filter only short-circuits emission, not arg construction. Cheap per call but multiplied by thousands of events per session. If this is permanent, consider `if logging.IsDebugEnabled() { ... }` gating; if it's truly a temporary trace (the run_multi.go comment says \"enables future regression checks of the bug\"), drop it before merging and recover it from git history when needed.\n\n### Style / project conventions\n\n- New code follows the project's \"no comments unless WHY is non-obvious\" rule well — the comments on `UsePTY`, `ptyReader`, `isEIO`, and `closePTY` all explain *why* (Linux EIO, parent-side slave close, fd lifecycle) rather than *what*.\n- `ReviewerTemplate.openStdout` returning `(io.Reader, *os.File, *os.File, error)` is awkward — three `*os.File` returns where two are nil in most cases invite mistakes at call sites. Consider a small `stdoutSetup` struct or returning a closer func, e.g. `(io.Reader, func(), error)` where the closer encapsulates the PTY teardown semantics.\n- `pty.Setsize` error is silently ignored with `//nolint:errcheck`. Fine, but consider `logging.Debug` on failure so it's at least observable.\n- New tests use `skipOnWindows(t, reason)` helper — nice refactor of the duplicated `runtime.GOOS == \"windows\"` blocks.\n\n### Test coverage\n\n- `TestReviewerTemplate_UsePTYPipesLinesEndToEnd` spawns a real `sh -c` and asserts five events arrive in order with a 5s timeout. Good.\n- `TestReviewerTemplate_UsePTYAppendsNoDecorationEnv` uses a *minimal* env (`PATH=/usr/bin:/bin`) and asserts the three vars get injected. This passes but is misleading — it doesn't exercise the production path where `os.Environ()` already contains `TERM`/`FORCE_COLOR`. Add a sibling test that seeds `cmd.Env` with `TERM=xterm-256color` and asserts that the child sees the *parent's* value, not `dumb`, then update the doc/comment accordingly (or change the behavior and update both).\n- No test for the `pty.ErrUnsupported` fallback path; the comment notes other tests cover the pipe path via `UsePTY=false`, which is fair but doesn't pin the *fallback* branch from `openStdout` specifically. A unit-level seam (interface around `pty.Open`) would be ideal but is probably overkill for one branch.\n- No test for the EIO-to-EOF translation in isolation. `isEIO` could get one quick table test (bare `syscall.EIO`, `*os.PathError` wrapping i